WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.250 align:start position:0%
 
do  daylight tries to spook you out but this  old dog really needed some new tricks  this first-person survival game is  bogged down by poor writing and endless  cliches you might be startled a few  times but you can only see so many  drawers fly out of bureaus before you  know when and where the so-called  surprises will occur daylight is  procedurally generated but it's no less  predictable than a typical linear  adventure  it just recycles the same basic frights  in different places  turning the initial scares into tepid  shrugs you could think of daylight as a  combination of slender the arrival and  outlast the slender comparison comes  from how you must escape each area  without coming face-to-face with ghostly  stalkers the outlast connection is the  setting which focuses on a now-defunct  asylum where terrible events once  occurred you move through each major  region as a young woman named Sarah  mapping progress on the cellphone you're  always holding you come across memos  personal Diaries and other written  remnants of the past collect enough of  these remnants and you may proceed to  the next stage of your journey there's a  story to piece together hear a story  partially driven by the insidious  disembodied voice that accompanies you  it takes several playthroughs for that  story to come into better focus but the  silly payoff isn't worth the tedium of  getting there  given the bad dialogue it's tempting to  presume that daylight is aiming for  b-movie appeal but the game isn't bad in  the good way it's just bad life is but a  butterfly's dream remarks your unseen  guide doing his best to make silly  nonsense sound like a deep thought Sara  says out of the blue you can't escape  destiny proving that she's watched  plenty of movies but adding nothing to  the story she's actually a part of by  the time a newspaper clipping raises the  possibility that a construction project  was built on a Native American burial  ground  you can only laugh daylight borrows all  sorts of ideas but does nothing to  enhance them like a discount Halloween  scary sound CD there are some nice  atmospheric touches like the rapid  flutter you hear when of pursuing which  approaches when your pursuer draws close  you can burn away her presence with a  flare which leads to a few decent  frights as you quickly reach for your  flare button if such details are  overshadowed by daylight's bizarre  randomness and dated visuals Sara will  scream in response to nothing at all  or comments on events in ways that make  absolutely no sense the game is most  interesting facet is the way it allows  you to connect the game to your twitch  TV channel but that feature only  contributes to the sense of sloppiness  viewers can type keywords into the chat  window and trigger a few scares of their  own in the game hearing a panicked cry  because somebody in your channel typed  scream as a curiosity but only a  curiosity who is it that screaming and  what exactly is she afraid of daylight  doesn't care screams are scary and  that's all that matters  take daylights claims to procedural  generation with a grain of salt while  the corridor mazes change somewhat from  one playthrough to the next layouts  remain consistent enough that you can  easily rush through them when making  return visits and yes indeed  daylight lets you sprint at full speed  all the time which destroys any sense of  tension given its procedural generation  and twitch integration daylight seems to  invite multiple playthroughs the irony  here is that it isn't worth playing  through even once

